{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"dynamic-memory","title":"Dynamic Memory: new & delete","group":"Pointers, Arrays & Strings","order":22,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>new</code> allocates an object on the heap and constructs it; <code>delete</code> destroys it and frees the memory. Every <code>new</code> needs exactly one matching <code>delete</code> — which is why modern C++ hides both inside smart pointers and containers.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>Syntax</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    int* n = new int(42);                  // one int\n    std::string* s = new std::string(\"heap\");\n    int* arr = new int[4]{1, 2, 3, 4};     // array\n\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; *n &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; *s &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; s-&gt;size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; arr[3] &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    delete n;          // single object → delete\n    delete s;\n    delete[] arr;      // array → delete[]\n    n = nullptr;       // avoid a dangling pointer\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>42 heap 4 4</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>malloc</code> / <code>free</code> (C)</th><th><code>new</code> / <code>delete</code> (C++)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Constructs/destroys objects</td><td>No — raw bytes</td><td>Yes — calls constructor / destructor</td></tr><tr><td>Returns</td><td><code>void*</code> (needs a cast in C++)</td><td>Typed <code>T*</code></td></tr><tr><td>On failure</td><td>Returns <code>NULL</code></td><td>Throws <code>std::bad_alloc</code> (or <code>nullptr</code> with <code>new (std::nothrow)</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>You compute bytes</td><td>Computed from the type</td></tr><tr><td>Can be overloaded</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (<code>operator new</code>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bug\"></i>The classic bugs</h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-droplet\"></i>Memory leak</h3><p>Forgetting <code>delete</code> (or an early <code>return</code>/exception skipping it). Memory is never reclaimed.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy-x\"></i>Double delete</h3><p>Deleting the same pointer twice — heap corruption.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-unlink\"></i>Use after free</h3><p>Using memory after <code>delete</code> — a dangling pointer.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-diff\"></i>Mismatched forms</h3><p><code>new[]</code> with <code>delete</code> (or <code>new</code> with <code>delete[]</code>) — undefined behaviour.</p></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++ — use after free</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nint main() {\n    int* p = new int(7);\n    delete p;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; *p &lt;&lt; '\\n';   // AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++ — mismatched delete</figcaption><pre><code>int main() {\n    int* arr = new int[10];\n    delete arr;                // clang -Wall rejects it; at run time ASan reports alloc-dealloc-mismatch\n}</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-shield-check\"></i>Leaks via exceptions — and the fix</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;memory&gt;\n#include &lt;stdexcept&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nvoid risky(bool fail) {\n    // int* buf = new int[100];  ... delete[] buf;  ← leaks if anything in between throws\n    auto buf = std::make_unique&lt;int[]&gt;(100);   // freed automatically, even on exceptions\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; better(100);              // or simply use a container\n    buf[0] = better[0] = 1;\n    if (fail) throw std::runtime_error(\"boom\");\n}\n\nint main() {\n    try { risky(true); } catch (const std::exception&amp; e) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"caught \" &lt;&lt; e.what() &lt;&lt; \", no leak\\n\"; }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>caught boom, no leak</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Use <code>std::vector</code>, <code>std::string</code>, <code>std::make_unique</code>, <code>std::make_shared</code> — they own memory and free it in their destructors (RAII).</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Writing <code>new</code>/<code>delete</code> in application code. If you see a raw <code>delete</code>, ask who else might delete or use that pointer.</p></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-grid-dots\"></i>2-D dynamic arrays</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nint main() {\n    int rows = 3, cols = 4;\n    std::vector&lt;std::vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; grid(rows, std::vector&lt;int&gt;(cols, 0));   // no new/delete needed\n    grid[2][3] = 9;\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; flat(rows * cols);                                  // one block: faster\n    flat[2 * cols + 3] = 9;                                              // row-major index\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; grid[2][3] &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; flat[11] &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>9 9</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What's the difference between delete and delete[]?</summary><div><p><code>delete p</code> destroys one object; <code>delete[] p</code> destroys every element of an array allocated with <code>new T[n]</code> (the runtime stores the count) and frees the block. Mixing them is undefined behaviour.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What happens if new fails?</summary><div><p>Plain <code>new</code> throws <code>std::bad_alloc</code>; it never returns null. <code>new (std::nothrow) T</code> returns <code>nullptr</code> instead. Checking <code>if (p == nullptr)</code> after plain <code>new</code> is dead code.</p></div></details></section>"}